<h1>STAR Interview Guide for Hiring Managers</h1>
<h2>Before the Interview</h2>
<ul><li>[ ] Review the role's competency table (4-6 competencies + weights)</li><li>[ ] Confirm your assigned competencies (for panel: each interviewer covers 1-2)</li><li>[ ] Prepare 2-3 STAR questions per competency from the question bank</li><li>[ ] Review candidate's resume — note any claims to probe in STAR format</li><li>[ ] Block 15 min post-interview for scorecard completion (do it immediately)</li><li>[ ] Send candidate the STAR prep brief 24 hours before</li></ul>
<hr/>
<h2>During the Interview (55-minute format)</h2>
<h3>Opening (5 min)</h3>
<ul><li>Introduce yourself and your role</li><li>Explain the format: &quot;I'll ask about specific past experiences. I'll take notes.&quot;</li><li>Set expectation: &quot;I may ask follow-up questions to understand your specific contribution.&quot;</li></ul>
<h3>STAR Questions (40 min)</h3>
<p>For each question:</p>
<ol><li>Ask the behavioral question (past-tense, open-ended)</li><li>Listen for Situation — if missing, probe: &quot;Can you give me more context on when this was?&quot;</li><li>Listen for Task — if missing: &quot;What was your specific responsibility?&quot;</li><li>Listen for Action — if &quot;we&quot; only: &quot;What did YOU personally do?&quot;</li><li>Listen for Result — always close: &quot;What was the end result?&quot; / &quot;Can you quantify that?&quot;</li><li>Take verbatim notes — do not interpret, just capture</li></ol>
<p><strong>80/20 rule:</strong> Candidate talks 80%, you talk 20%. If you're talking more, stop asking follow-ups.</p>
<h3>Candidate Questions (10 min)</h3>
<ul><li>Answer honestly — never oversell</li><li>If asked about compensation or offer: &quot;That's handled by our recruiting team&quot;</li></ul>
<h3>Closing (5 min)</h3>
<ul><li>Explain next steps and timeline</li><li>Thank them — every candidate is a potential customer or referrer</li></ul>
<hr/>
<h2>After the Interview</h2>
<ul><li>[ ] Complete scorecard within 30 minutes (before memory fades)</li><li>[ ] Submit scorecard before checking Slack, Teams, or email from other interviewers</li><li>[ ] Do NOT discuss the candidate until all scorecards are submitted</li><li>[ ] If you have a strong signal either direction: document the specific evidence, not the feeling</li></ul>
<hr/>
<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Why it hurts</th><th>Instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asking &quot;How would you...&quot;</td><td>Hypothetical — no predictive value</td><td>&quot;Tell me about a time when...&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Accepting &quot;we did X&quot; as Action</td><td>Re-attribution — can't score</td><td>&quot;What did YOU specifically do?&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting to ask for Result</td><td>Incomplete STAR</td><td>Always close: &quot;What was the outcome?&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Discussing before everyone submits</td><td>Anchoring bias</td><td>Wait for all scorecards</td></tr><tr><td>Scoring delivery, not content</td><td>Fluency bias</td><td>Focus on S/T/A/R completeness and evidence</td></tr></tbody></table>
